{#
    `.. topic::` as the reStructuredText parser builds it — the spelling an
    author writes, and a different node from the one `body/topic` renders.
    Both have an override because both are reachable. A theme that covers one
    of them looks right until somebody writes the other.

    Same shape, same reason: see `body/topic.html.twig` for why an `<aside>`
    on `.sds-panel` and not an admonition.

    The classes from the source come along. An author who wrote `:class:` on a
    topic meant it for their own stylesheet. To drop it silently is the one
    thing a theme must not do with what it does not understand.
#}
<aside class="sds-topic sds-panel{% if node.classes %} {{ node.classesString }}{% endif %}">
    <p class="sds-topic__title">{{ renderNode(node.content) }}</p>
    {{ renderNode(node.value) }}
</aside>
